## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates blending two classic sorts to optimize real workloads and caching behavior, plus reasoning about stability and memory trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Hybrid sorting (Introsort-like) with depth-limited quicksort
- Threshold tuning for switching to mergesort
- Stability in final output and how to preserve it
- Complexity: worst-case O(n log n) via depth cap, average-case O(n log n); extra space for merges

## Code Example

```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, lo=0, hi=arr.length-1, depth=0) {
  const n = hi - lo + 1;
  if (n <= 32) return stableMergeSort(arr, lo, hi);
  if (depth > 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(arr.length))) return heapSort(arr, lo, hi);
  const p = partition(arr, lo, hi);
  hybridSort(arr, lo, p-1, depth+1);
  hybridSort(arr, p+1, hi, depth+1);
  return arr;
}
```

